---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro'
import { href } from '../../lib/url'

const example = `import std.io { println }

struct User {
    name: String
    age: Int
}

fn describe(user: User) -> String {
    return user.name
}

fn main() {
    let user = User { name: "Ada", age: 36 }
    var total = 0
    for i in 0..3 {
        total += i
    }
    println(describe(user))
    println(total)
}`
---
<SiteLayout title="Nova compiles itself now" path="blog/building-nova-in-rust" description="How Nova moved to a self-hosted compiler and language server, what the native pipeline does today, and what remains.">
  <article class="prose blog-post">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span> Migration note · November 2026</span>
      <h1>Nova compiles<br />itself <span style="color: var(--yellow)">now.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">The compiler, language server, formatter, diagnostics and package manager are Nova programs. <code>bootstrap/nova</code> is a Nova-built compiler that rebuilds the toolchain, and a fresh checkout needs no other language toolchain.</p>
      <div class="hero-pill"><span>Self-hosted compiler + LSP</span></div>
      <p class="notice"><strong>November 2026:</strong> the original bootstrap compiler was removed. Source, tests and CI are Nova-only; the frozen goldens preserve the old compiler's accepted programs and diagnostics.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="wrap">
      <div class="section">
        <h2>One pipeline, one language</h2>
        <p>Nova is not an interpreter with a pretty editor attached. It is a statically typed language whose compiler lexes, parses, checks and emits LLVM IR, then links a real executable. Every stage is now implemented in Nova:</p>
        <pre><code>bootstrap/nova        checked-in Nova-built bootstrap
libraries/
  nova-lexer          tokens and spans
  nova-parser         recursive descent + Pratt parsing
  nova-check          declarations and body semantics
  nova-codegen        textual LLVM IR + Clang
  nova-lsp            stdio language server
  nova-fmt            formatter
  nova-diag           diagnostic rendering
  nova-boxes          package manager
examples/nova-tool    the `nova` CLI
runtime/              C runtime and native GUI host</code></pre>
        <p>The C runtime and the host Clang remain because LLVM and the platform linker are external tools. They are not a second compiler implementation, and no step requires another language toolchain.</p>

        <h2>The compiler builds itself</h2>
        <p><code>bootstrap/nova</code> compiles <code>examples/nova-tool</code> into a new compiler. That compiler rebuilds itself, and the generations emit byte-identical IR. The same binary runs the CLI and the language server, so the editor and the command line cannot drift into separate front ends.</p>
        <p>The self-build is also the regression gate: the full compiler source parses in about a quarter of a second, and all eleven suites run from the checked-in bootstrap without a second toolchain.</p>

        <h2>The first working vertical slice</h2>
        <p>A Nova program can use immutable and mutable bindings, functions, structs, lists, strings, <code>Option</code>, <code>Result</code>, <code>match</code>, loops and ranges. This program compiles to a native binary and prints real output:</p>
        <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>
        <p>That is deliberately a slice, not a finished language. The suites cover the lexer, parser, semantic checker, native code generation, package manager, CLI and language server: 248 codegen assertions, 18 frozen native programs, 27 frozen diagnostics, 64 package assertions and 126 toolchain assertions.</p>

        <h2>Studio is part of the language, not a separate demo</h2>
        <p>Nova Studio is an Electron workbench built around Monaco, React and strict TypeScript. It speaks to the same Nova LSP and runs the same CLI. Diagnostics, completion, hover, definition and formatting are not emulated in the renderer.</p>
        <p>The workbench also owns the unglamorous parts: workspace-confined file operations, atomic saves with external-change checks, argv-based task execution, a terminal, Vim mode and a FreeBuff terminal. The FreeBuff boundary is explicit: the installed CLI exposes an interactive terminal, not a documented structured chat protocol, so Nova does not scrape it or pretend to have ghost text.</p>
        <p>Recent work added a small, fully local visual-effects layer: a dock-style current-line lens, typing and scroll sparkles, and synthesized Web Audio feedback. It is off-switchable in settings, respects reduced motion and never captures editor input. It is a visual lens, not a per-line font-size change, so caret geometry stays stable.</p>

        <h2>What “working” does not mean</h2>
        <p>Nova is a functioning native language toolchain today, but it is not a complete mainstream language. The honest gaps:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>No generics, traits, methods or closures yet.</li>
          <li>Map/set literals and iteration are incomplete; sema covers less than a full type system.</li>
          <li><code>nova test</code> and the REPL are not ported yet.</li>
          <li>The browser playground uses checked-in WebAssembly until a Nova wasm backend exists.</li>
          <li>No package registry publishing, signed/notarized installers or benchmark claim.</li>
          <li>The GUI has typecheck/build coverage but not an automated end-to-end interaction suite.</li>
          <li>macOS arm64 is the tested platform; Linux and Windows builds are not yet verified end to end.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Those gaps are in the <a href={href('roadmap')}>roadmap</a> and feature status, not hidden in a launch post. The correct claim is narrow and strong: Nova is a self-hosted native compiler pipeline with a working editor and documented limits.</p>

        <h2>Try it from source</h2>
        <p>You need the checked-in bootstrap and a C linker. Node is only required for Studio and this website.</p>
        <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/jd4rider/nova.git
cd nova
(cd examples/nova-tool && ../../bootstrap/nova build)
bootstrap/nova new hello
cd hello
../bootstrap/nova run</code></pre>
        <p>Then open the editor with <code>NOVA_BIN="$PWD/../bootstrap/nova" npm run dev</code> from <code>editor/</code>. The full setup, including the optional <code>node-pty</code> terminal and the FreeBuff limitation, is in the <a href={href('getting-started/installation/')}>installation guide</a>.</p>

        <div class="cta" style="margin-top: 40px;">
          <div>
            <div class="kicker">The source is the claim</div>
            <h2>Read the pipeline.</h2>
            <p>Compiler, CLI, language server, IDE and documentation live in one repository.</p>
          </div>
          <a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/jd4rider/nova" rel="noreferrer">Open GitHub ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
